// №431 direct repro (окно p431-late-cancel-noabort): calls the runtime
// function at the ACTUAL defect location (`nova_throw_cancel`,
// `compiler-codegen/nova_rt/effects.h`) with `_nova_fail_top == NULL` —
// the exact precondition documented for all 8 registry call sites — via a
// TEMPORARY FFI hook (`nova_p431_direct_repro`, `compiler-codegen/nova_rt/
// effects.c`), instead of trying to win an organic scheduler race.
//
// WHY a direct hook and not an organic .nv-level race: this window tried
// two organic scenarios — (1) `tok.cancel()` called BEFORE `supervised
// (cancel: tok) { spawn {...} }` even starts (a deterministic-looking
// "cancel-before-bind" shape derived from reading `nova_cancel_token_bind`,
// `fibers.h`), and (2) the historically-crashing mass-cancel fixture
// (`spec_tests/conformance/standalone/pos_max_fibers_concurrent.nv`, the
// `[fix-boehm-eager-cost]` wave's carrier, independently re-confirmed
// clean 200/200 in `docs/plans/wip/PROGRESS-p427.md`). Neither reproduced
// in THIS codebase (see PROGRESS-p431.md for the full trace of why —
// `nova_preempt_check`, this window's first hypothesis for scenario 1,
// turned out to gate on the sysmon overrun flag, not cancellation,
// invalidating that specific mechanism). The registry's own framing (see
// 221.1 №431) is itself a proactive code-audit finding — a discrepancy
// with D75, not a live crash report — so a from-code, deterministic proof
// of the exact documented failure state is the honest, defensible
// evidence available within this window's budget.
//
// PRE-FIX:  process aborts — stderr prints "nova: cancel-throw outside
//   any supervised scope: ...".
// POST-FIX: process exits 0 — stderr prints the late-cancel summary line
//   (`_nova_late_cancel_atexit_print`, effects.c).
module nova_repro.p431_direct_repro

extern "C" fn nova_p431_direct_repro() -> ()

fn main() {
    nova_p431_direct_repro()
    println("harness: survived nova_throw_cancel with no handler")
}
